1. What is Express?
    Express is a lightweight web framework built on top of Node.js.
    Node gives us low-level HTTP capabilities:
        http.createServer()
    Express gives us higher-level abstractions:
        app.get()
        app.post()
        app.use()
        app.listen()
    Conceptually:
        Node.js
        │
        └── HTTP module
                │
                ▼
            Express
                │
                ▼
            Your REST API
    Express.js is a lightweight web framework for Node.js that provides abstractions for routing, middleware, request handling, and responses, making it easier to build HTTP APIs and web applications.


2. Installing Express
    Inside your project:
        npm install express
    Create:
        src/
        └── server.js
    Then:
        const express = require("express");
        const app = express();
        app.listen(3000, () => {
            console.log("Server running on port 3000");
        });
    Run:
        node src/server.js
    Your server is now listening on:
        http://localhost:3000


3. Your first Express route
        const express = require("express");
        const app = express();
        app.get("/", (req, res) => {
            res.send("Hello from Express");
        });
        app.listen(3000);
    Now:
        GET /
    returns:
        Hello from Express
    Compare this with raw Node:
        if (req.method === "GET" && req.url === "/") {
            res.end("Hello");
        }
    Express simplifies it to:
        app.get("/", handler);


4. HTTP methods
    Express provides methods corresponding to HTTP methods:
        app.get();
        app.post();
        app.put();
        app.patch();
        app.delete();
    Example:
        app.get("/users", (req, res) => {
            res.send("Get users");
        });

        app.post("/users", (req, res) => {
            res.send("Create user");
        });

        app.put("/users/:id", (req, res) => {
            res.send("Update user");
        });

        app.delete("/users/:id", (req, res) => {
            res.send("Delete user");
        });
    This is much cleaner than manually inspecting:
        req.method
        req.url


5. Route parameters
    Suppose:
        GET /users/123
    You define:
        app.get("/users/:id", (req, res) => {
            console.log(req.params.id);

            res.send(`User ID: ${req.params.id}`);
        });
    Output:
        User ID: 123
    The :id is a route parameter.
    Multiple parameters:
        app.get("/users/:userId/orders/:orderId", (req, res) => {
            console.log(req.params.userId);
            console.log(req.params.orderId);
        });
    Request:
        /users/10/orders/500
    gives:
        userId = 10
        orderId = 500


6. Query parameters
    Request:
        GET /users?page=2&limit=10
    Access them using:
        app.get("/users", (req, res) => {
            console.log(req.query.page);
            console.log(req.query.limit);

            res.send("OK");
        });
    Output:
        2
        10
    Important distinction:
        /users/123
        ↑
        req.params
    versus:
        /users?page=2
            ↑
        req.query
    Interview question
    Q: Difference between req.params and req.query?
        req.params contains dynamic values defined in the route path, while req.query contains query-string parameters appended to the URL.


7. Request body
    Suppose client sends:
        {
            "name": "John",
            "email": "john@example.com"
        }
    You need:
        app.use(express.json());
    Then:
        app.post("/users", (req, res) => {
            console.log(req.body);

            res.json({
                message: "User received",
                user: req.body
            });
        });
    Now:
        req.body
    contains:
        {
            name: "John",
            email: "john@example.com"
        }


8. What does express.json() actually do?
    When the client sends JSON:
        HTTP Request
            │
            ▼
        JSON body
            │
            ▼
        express.json()
            │
            ▼
        req.body
    It is middleware that parses incoming JSON request bodies and makes the parsed data available through:
        req.body
    Without it, depending on the request and Express setup, req.body may be undefined.


9. Middleware — the heart of Express
    Middleware is a function that has access to:
        req
        res
        next
    Example:
        app.use((req, res, next) => {
            console.log("Request received");

            next();
        });
    The important function is:
        next();
    It tells Express:
        "I'm done with my middleware processing. Continue to the next matching middleware or route."


0. Middleware request flow
    Suppose:
        app.use((req, res, next) => {
            console.log("Middleware 1");
            next();
        });

        app.use((req, res, next) => {
            console.log("Middleware 2");
            next();
        });

        app.get("/", (req, res) => {
            console.log("Controller");
            res.send("Hello");
        });
    Request:
        GET /
    Output:
        Middleware 1
        Middleware 2
        Controller
    The flow is:
        Request
        ↓
        Middleware 1
        ↓ next()
        Middleware 2
        ↓ next()
        Route handler
        ↓
        Response


11. What happens if you don't call next()?
    Consider:
        app.use((req, res, next) => {
            console.log("Middleware");
        });
    There's no:
        next();
    and no response:
        res.send()
    The request can remain hanging.
    Conceptually:
        Request
        ↓
        Middleware
        ↓
        ❌
    Nothing tells Express to continue.
    This is one of the most common middleware mistakes.


12. Middleware can terminate a request
    next() isn't always required.
    For authentication:
        app.use((req, res, next) => {

            const token = req.headers.authorization;

            if (!token) {
                return res.status(401).json({
                    message: "Unauthorized"
                });
            }

            next();
        });
    If there's no token:
        Request
        ↓
        Auth middleware
        ↓
        401 Response
    The request doesn't continue.
    If authentication succeeds:
        Request
        ↓
        Auth middleware
        ↓ next()
        Controller


13. Real-world middleware
    Typical backend middleware:
        Request
        │
        ▼
        CORS
        │
        ▼
        JSON parser
        │
        ▼
        Logger
        │
        ▼
        Authentication
        │
        ▼
        Validation
        │
        ▼
        Controller
        │
        ▼
        Response
    This is why Express middleware is so powerful.


14. Application-level middleware
    Example:
        app.use((req, res, next) => {
            console.log(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);
            next();
        });
    This runs for matching requests across the application.
    You can also restrict it:
        app.use("/admin", (req, res, next) => {
            console.log("Admin request");
            next();
        });
    This middleware applies to /admin routes.


15. Router-level middleware
    As your application grows, you don't want everything in app.js.
    Create:
        routes/
        └── userRoutes.js

        const express = require("express");

        const router = express.Router();

        router.get("/", (req, res) => {
            res.json({
                message: "Get users"
            });
        });

        router.post("/", (req, res) => {
            res.json({
                message: "Create user"
            });
        });

        module.exports = router;
    Then:
        const userRoutes = require("./routes/userRoutes");
        app.use("/users", userRoutes);
    Now:
        GET  /users
        POST /users
    are handled by userRoutes.


16. Why routers are important
    A production backend might have:
        routes/
        ├── userRoutes.js
        ├── authRoutes.js
        ├── orderRoutes.js
        ├── productRoutes.js
        └── paymentRoutes.js
    Then:
        app.use("/users", userRoutes);
        app.use("/auth", authRoutes);
        app.use("/orders", orderRoutes);
        app.use("/products", productRoutes);
        app.use("/payments", paymentRoutes);
    This gives you modular routing.


17. Controller vs Service
    This is where you should start thinking like a backend developer.
    Don't do this:
        app.post("/users", async (req, res) => {

            // validation

            // database query

            // password hashing

            // email

            // business logic

            // response
        });
    Instead:
        Route
        ↓
        Controller
        ↓
        Service
        ↓
        Repository / Database
    Example:
        Route:
            router.post("/", createUser);
        Controller:
            async function createUser(req, res) {
                const user = await userService.createUser(req.body);
                res.status(201).json(user);
            }
        Service:
            async function createUser(data) {
                // business logic
                // database interaction
            }
    This separation becomes extremely important in interviews.


18. Express error handling
    Suppose:
        app.get("/users", async (req, res) => {

            throw new Error("Database failed");

        });
    You need centralized error handling.
    Express error middleware has four arguments:
        app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
            console.error(err);

            res.status(500).json({
                message: "Internal Server Error"
            });
        });
    The four arguments are critical:
        (err, req, res, next)
    Express recognizes this as error-handling middleware based on the four-argument signature.


19. Why centralized error handling?
    Without centralized handling, you might have:
        try {
            ...
        } catch (error) {
            res.status(500).json(...);
        }
    in every controller.
    Instead:
        Controller
            ↓
        Error
            ↓
        next(error)
            ↓
        Central error middleware
            ↓
        Response
    Example:
        app.get("/users", async (req, res, next) => {
            try {
                const users = await getUsers();

                res.json(users);
            } catch (error) {
                next(error);
            }
        });
    Then:
        app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
            res.status(500).json({
                message: err.message
            });
        });


20. Middleware order matters
    Consider:
        app.use(authMiddleware);
        app.get("/users", getUsers);
    Authentication runs before /users.
    But:
        app.get("/users", getUsers);
        app.use(authMiddleware);
    The auth middleware won't protect that route because the request already matched the route and generated a response.
    Similarly, your error middleware should generally be registered after your routes.
    Conceptually:
        app
        │
        ├── body parser
        ├── logger
        ├── authentication
        ├── routes
        └── error handler
    Order is significant.


21. Build a realistic Express application
    Structure:
        src/
        │
        ├── server.js
        ├── app.js
        │
        ├── routes/
        │   └── userRoutes.js
        │
        ├── controllers/
        │   └── userController.js
        │
        ├── services/
        │   └── userService.js
        │
        └── middleware/
            └── errorMiddleware.js
    app.js:
        const express = require("express");

        const userRoutes = require("./routes/userRoutes");
        const errorMiddleware = require("./middleware/errorMiddleware");

        const app = express();

        app.use(express.json());

        app.use("/users", userRoutes);

        app.use(errorMiddleware);

        module.exports = app;
    server.js:
        const app = require("./app");

        app.listen(3000, () => {
            console.log("Server running on port 3000");
        });
    Notice:
        app.js:    configures the application.
        server.js:    starts the HTTP server.
    That's a clean separation.


22. Interview questions
    Q1. What is Express?
        Express is a lightweight Node.js web framework that provides routing, middleware, request handling, and response abstractions for building web applications and APIs.

    Q2. What is middleware?
        Middleware is a function that executes during the request-response lifecycle and has access to req, res, and next. It can modify the request or response, terminate the request, or pass control to the next middleware.

    Q3. What does next() do?
        next() passes control to the next matching middleware or route handler.

    Q4. What happens if middleware doesn't call next() or send a response?
        The request can remain pending because Express has no instruction to continue processing or complete the response.

    Q5. Difference between req.params, req.query, and req.body?
        req.params contains route parameters, req.query contains URL query parameters, and req.body contains parsed request body data.

    Q6. What is express.json()?
        It's built-in Express middleware that parses incoming JSON request bodies and makes the resulting object available through req.body.

    Q7. What is error-handling middleware?
        Middleware with the signature (err, req, res, next) that handles errors centrally in the Express request pipeline.

    Q8. Why does middleware order matter?
        Express processes middleware in registration order, so authentication, parsing, routes, and error handling need to be registered in the appropriate sequence.

    Q9. Node HTTP module vs Express?
        Node's HTTP module provides low-level HTTP functionality, while Express builds higher-level abstractions such as routing and middleware on top of Node.


23. Practical exercise
    Build this:
        GET    /users
        GET    /users/:id
        POST   /users
        PATCH  /users/:id
        DELETE /users/:id
    Use:
        routes
        controllers
        services
    Structure it as:
        src/
        ├── app.js
        ├── server.js
        ├── routes/
        │   └── userRoutes.js
        ├── controllers/
        │   └── userController.js
        ├── services/
        │   └── userService.js
        └── middleware/
            └── errorMiddleware.js
    Use an in-memory array initially. Don't introduce MongoDB yet.
    The purpose is to make sure you understand the complete flow:
        HTTP Request
            ↓
        Express
            ↓
        Middleware
            ↓
        Router
            ↓
        Controller
            ↓
        Service
            ↓
        Response